{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And Judah said: Bring her forth, and let her be burnt.</b> Ephraim the student of Rabbi Meir said in the name of Rabbi Meir: Tamar was the daughter of Shem, who was a priest; therefore they judged her by burning (Genesis Rabbah 85:10). This is what Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 38:24), and he did not explain it. And I do not know this ruling, for a daughter of a priest is not liable to burning except for committing adultery while bound to a husband — whether betrothed or fully married — as is explicitly stated in the Gemara in Sanhedrin (51b). But a daughter of a priest who is a *shomeret yavam* (waiting for levirate marriage) and commits adultery is not liable to any death penalty at all, and whether she is a daughter of an Israelite or a daughter of a priest, she is only under a negative prohibition. And if you should say that yibbum (levirate marriage) was practiced among the children of Noah and she was in their category of a married woman, and their prohibition is punished by death (Sanhedrin 57b) — but do they not themselves say (Genesis Rabbah 85:5) that Judah was the first to begin the practice of yibbum? And likewise in the Gemara in Sanhedrin (57b), it is proven that a *yevamah* among the children of Noah is not liable at all. And it appears to me that Judah was a magistrate, an officer, and a ruler in the land, and the daughter-in-law who commits adultery against him is not judged according to the law of ordinary people, but rather as one who insults the sovereign authority. Therefore it is written, 'And Judah said: Bring her forth, and let her be burnt,' for they came before him to do with her according to all that he would command, and he sentenced her to death on account of the dignity of royalty, and he judged her as one who profanes her father by virtue of his priesthood — not that the law of ordinary people would be so. And according to the plain sense of Scripture, it is possible that their law was as is customary today in some lands of Sefarad, that a woman who commits adultery under her husband is handed over to her husband, and he judges her to death or to life as he pleases. And behold, she was designated for Shelah his son, and she was in their eyes as a married woman according to their customs."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:12.847462036Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}